# The reviewer /ship runs for step 7, the security pass, when the gate is a
# separate one. A combined gate has no step 7: its one review covers the
# security areas too, and this value goes unused on that branch. Two shapes:
# agent:<name> dispatches that agent on this host, and anything else is a
# command line the audit prompt is appended to.
#
# `auto` means Codex with gpt-5.6-sol, read-only, in a fresh session, told not to
# read the reviewed repository's own instructions. That is the one reviewer that
# has been put to the test: it was run against the fixtures in this plugin's
# tests/fixtures/security-review, found both planted Critical defects with the
# attacker, the input and the gain, and invented nothing against the clean
# counterpart. Nothing else here has been qualified, so nothing else is chosen
# for you. A host without codex stops the gate and names your file, rather than
# running an unqualified reviewer and calling the result a security pass.
#
# If Sol is refused with an HTTP 400, /ship retries once with gpt-5.6-terra and
# says in the pull request which reviewer actually ran. A review that silently
# downgraded is worse than one that did not happen.
#
# No agent is chosen automatically. An agent is dispatched by bare name into the
# repository being reviewed, which can hold a definition of that name, and the
# gate cannot tell which of the two it would run. You may still state
# agent:<name> in your own file; the gate then refuses if the repository being
# reviewed holds .claude/agents/<name>.md, so remove that file from the worktree
# or name a command line instead. Only Claude Code can dispatch an agent; on any
# other harness an agent: value stops the gate rather than running something
# else.
#
# This file is replaced whenever the skill is updated, so do not edit it. To
# change the reviewer, copy it to ~/.config/ship/security-reviewer, or to
# $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/ship/security-reviewer when that is set, and edit the copy:
# /ship reads that file first and falls back to this one.
auto
